{"name":"Amulet Gem","hex":"#01748e","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"amulet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A religious article, protective charm, or ornament, usually bearing cultural or magical symbols, worn for protection against ill will, negative influences, the evil eye, or evil spirits."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/amulet"},{"term":"gem","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-5 language code for Germanic languages."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A precious stone, usually of substantial monetary value or prized for its beauty or shine.","Any precious or highly valued thing or person."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gem"}],"entries":[{"title":"Amulet","blocks":[{"text":"An amulet is a spiritual object believed to confer protection or grace upon its possessor. The word amulet comes from the Latin word amuletum, which Pliny's Natural History describes as \"an object that protects a person from trouble\". Anything can function as an amulet; items commonly so used include statues, coins, drawings, plant parts, animal parts, and written words. The word phylactery is sometimes used as a general synonym likewise referring to any unspecified amulet, but also has a specific definition within Judaism. Certain amulets may also qualify more specifically as a devotional article, good luck charm, or even both in rare circumstances, but those categories represent only subsets of amulets."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Cheshm-Nazar.JPG/330px-Cheshm-Nazar.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amulet","wikidata":"Q131557"},{"title":"Gemstone","blocks":[{"text":"A gemstone is a piece of mineral crystal which, when cut or polished, is used to make jewelry or other adornments. Certain rocks and occasionally organic materials that are not minerals may also be used for jewelry and are therefore often considered to be gemstones as well. Most gemstones are hard, but some softer minerals such as brazilianite may be used in jewelry because of their color, luster, or other physical properties that have aesthetic value. However, generally speaking, soft minerals are not typically used as gemstones by virtue of their brittleness and lack of durability."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Cardinal_gems.png/330px-Cardinal_gems.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemstone","wikidata":"Q83437"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Egyptian_-_Group_of_16_Amulets_Strung_as_a_Necklace_-_Walters_481685-1699_-_View_A.jpg/500px-Egyptian_-_Group_of_16_Amulets_Strung_as_a_Necklace_-_Walters_481685-1699_-_View_A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Egyptian_-_Group_of_16_Amulets_Strung_as_a_Necklace_-_Walters_481685-1699_-_View_A.jpg/1280px-Egyptian_-_Group_of_16_Amulets_Strung_as_a_Necklace_-_Walters_481685-1699_-_View_A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Egyptian_-_Group_of_16_Amulets_Strung_as_a_Necklace_-_Walters_481685-1699_-_View_A.jpg/500px-Egyptian_-_Group_of_16_Amulets_Strung_as_a_Necklace_-_Walters_481685-1699_-_View_A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Djed, wadj, and figures of gods; amulets made of Egyptian faience.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEgyptian_-_Group_of_16_Amulets_Strung_as_a_Necklace_-_Walters_481685-1699_-_View_A.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Barnstenen_amulet_korenaren_ValkenvburgZH_101064_RMO_Leiden.jpg/500px-Barnstenen_amulet_korenaren_ValkenvburgZH_101064_RMO_Lei